1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,326
Seven in ten online carts never check out.

2
00:00:03,366 --> 00:00:07,600
Cheapest Yes uses PayPal and AI to win
them back, cheaply.

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:11,052
Most stores answer with a blanket
discount.

4
00:00:11,092 --> 00:00:15,350
That hands margin to shoppers who would
have paid full price anyway.

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,966
Price isn't the only lever. On a $240
cart,

6
00:00:22,006 --> 00:00:27,501
PayPal Pay Later costs about $3.60 extra,
and only when it's used.

7
00:00:27,541 --> 00:00:29,050
10% off costs $24.

8
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:35,635
AI models score each shopper's session. A
profit engine picks one incentive,

9
00:00:35,675 --> 00:00:38,080
or none. PayPal runs the checkout.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,661
This shopper stalls at a $288 total. The
engine picks Pay Later,

11
00:00:45,701 --> 00:00:51,430
lifting the chance of a purchase from 20
to 73 percent, and shows its reasons.

12
00:01:00,990 --> 00:01:06,390
Then a real PayPal sandbox payment, using
a test card. No real money moves.

13
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:17,980
The capture and PayPal's fee are logged
with the decision.

14
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:27,516
A loyal customer, already 80 percent
likely to buy. The engine offers nothing,

15
00:01:27,556 --> 00:01:29,030
and keeps the margin.

16
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:41,511
A student comparing prices in other tabs.
Pay Later barely moves them,

17
00:01:41,551 --> 00:01:44,210
so here the cheapest yes is 10% off.

18
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:58,834
Every option is scored on expected profit
after fees. Prices never go above list,

19
00:01:58,874 --> 00:02:02,130
and budgets and per-shopper caps keep it
honest.

20
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:07,297
On 20,000 simulated shoppers,

21
00:02:07,337 --> 00:02:12,930
it beats a blanket discount by about
$8,300 per thousand shoppers.

22
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:18,235
Every decision lands in an AG Grid log
with its PayPal IDs,

23
00:02:18,275 --> 00:02:23,110
and a Gemini copilot answers the
merchant's questions from that log.

24
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:37,927
Honestly, always showing Pay Later gets
most of the way, and without Pay Later,

25
00:02:37,967 --> 00:02:43,147
profit drops $6,600 per thousand shoppers.
These are simulator results,

26
00:02:43,187 --> 00:02:44,510
not field results.

27
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:50,940
List price is the ceiling. Profit is the
goal. PayPal Pay Later is the cheapest yes.
